\pnlcltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl9\pnlcrm\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}\pard\plain \s15\qc\nowidctlpar\adjustright \f4 {\f1\fs32 Migrating Apache 1.3 Modules to Apache 2.0
\par }{\f1 Ryan Bloom
\par 
\par }\pard \s15\fi720\nowidctlpar\adjustright {
Apache's biggest benefit to users is that it is extensible.  The mechanism used for this extensibility is modules, segments of code that programmers can insert into the request processing to add functionality to Apache.  There are hundreds if not thousand
s of Apache modules, but they have all been written for Apache 1.3.  Unfortunately, without modification, these modules will not work with Apache 2.0.  This presentation will discuss those changes and why they}{\cf13  }{are needed}{\cf13 .}{
\par 
\par }\pard \s15\qc\fi720\nowidctlpar\adjustright {Module Hooks
\par 
\par }\pard \s15\fi720\nowidctlpar\adjustright {In its simplest form
, an Apache 1.3 module is a table of functions which the module implements.  The functions correspond to the place in the request processing that the module will get to execute code.  Each module does not need to implement every function, and most do not.
  The table looks like:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 module MODULE_VARIABLE = \{
\par     STANDARD_MODULE_STUFF,
\par     initializer
\par     dir config creater
\par     dir merger
\par     server config
\par     merge server config
\par     command table
\par     handlers
\par     filename translation
\par     check_user_id
\par     check auth
\par     check access
\par     type_checker
\par     fixups
\par     logger
\par     header parser
\par     child_init
\par     child_exit
\par     post read-request
\par \};
\par 
\par }{This module structure has been trimmed substantially in Apache 2.0.  The new module structure looks like:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 module  MODULE_VARIABLE= \{
\par     STANDARD20_MODULE_STUFF,
\par     dir config creator
\par     dir merger
\par     server config
\par     merge server config
\par     command ap_table_t
\par     handlers
\par     register hooks
\par \};
\par 
\par }{The most important change is the addition of the register hoo
ks function to the new module structure.  This new function replaces all of the functions that were removed.  The register hooks function allows a module to register a function to be called for any remaining hook.  For example, if mod_userdir needs to exe
cute some code during the translate name hook, in its register hooks function it calls
\par 
\par }{\fs20 ap_hook_translate_name(translate_userdir,  NULL, aszSucc, HOOK_MIDDLE);
\par 
\par }\pard \s15\nowidctlpar\adjustright {This call registers the translate_userdir function to be called during the translate name hook, just as the module required.  The remaining arguments will be discussed later in this presentation.
\par }\pard \s15\fi735\nowidctlpar\adjustright {Currently, the module structure is a combination of function pointers and the register hooks function.  The hope is that by the time Apache 2.0 is released,
 everything that can be registered in the register hooks function will be.  Currently, the valid places to register a hook are:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 ap_hook_post_config
\par ap_hook_open_logs
\par ap_hook_auth_checker
\par ap_hook_check_user_id
\par ap_hook_access_checker
\par ap_hook_child_init
\par ap_hook_fixups
\par ap_hook_log_transaction
\par ap_hook_post_read_request
\par ap_hook_translate_name
\par ap_hook_type_checker
\par }{
\par However, there are more differences between modules in Apache 1.3 and 2.0 than just how functions are registered.  In Apache 1.3, modules are mono
lithic, meaning that if mod_userdir is listed before mod_mime, then for every hook in the modules the mod_userdir function is called before the mod_mime function.  This has caused problems for some modules.  For example, it would be impossible to resove a
 
situation where a module needs to be the first module to check the user_id but the last to translate the url name.  This limitation is removed in Apache 2.0.  In Apache 2.0, each hook can be scheduled independently of the other hooks.  Every ap_hook_foo f
unction takes the same arguments: the name of the function to register, the predecessors, the successors, and the position.  The first argument is obvious, but the other three require a bit of explanation.
\par The predecessors and successors are complementary f
ields.  Each module function can register to have a specific relationship with the same function in a different module.  For example, mod_userdir requires that its translate name function be called before mod_alias's translate name function.  In order to 
d
o this, the module defines an array of character strings which in this case contains "mod_alias".  This array is passed in as the successors argument to ap_hook_translate_name.  The same can be done for predecessors.  An example of this can be found in mo
d_mmap_static.  
\par The final argument is the relative position of the module.  There are three generic values for this argument: HOOK_FIRST, HOOK_MIDDLE, and HOOK_LAST.  These provide a less fine-grained amount of control than the string array described in th
e previous paragraph.  Apache 2.0 guarantees that any hook specifying HOOK_FIRST will be called before a hook specifying HOOK_MIDDLE, which will be called before a hook which specifies HOOK_LAST.  Modules with no order requirements should use HOOK_MIDDLE.

\par The new hook architecture has another benefit for module authors.  In the past, whenever a new hook was added to the table, all of the modules needed to be modified and re-compiled.  This is no longer the case, because each module only references the hook
s that it actually uses.  As a result, when a new hook is added, it has no effect on pre-existing modules.
\par 
\par }\pard \s15\qc\nowidctlpar\adjustright {Initialization Routines
\par }\pard \s15\fi720\nowidctlpar\adjustright {
\par Apache 2.0 has removed the initialization routine from the module table and replaced it with two different functions, pre-co
nfig and post-config.  These two functions look similar, but should be used very differently. The pre-config hook is not called by dynamically allocated modules and should not be used by most modules.  So, when should the pre-config hook be used?  Apache 
2
.0 has added support for what are called Multi-Processing modules.  These are modules which cannot be dynamically loaded and which define how requests are mapped to execution primitives (either processes or threads).  The pre-config hook was provided for 
these modules.  
\par The pre-config hook is called before the config file is read, and the post-config hook is called after it has been read.  What is most important for module writers to know however is that the post-config hook has taken the place of the old 
initialization hook.
\par There has been one other change in the initialization hook.  The arguments to it have changed.  In Apache 1.3, the initialization hook took one pool and one server_rec variable.  This has changed to allow for more control.  The post-co
nfig hook now takes three ap_context_t's (these have replaced pools, as explained below), and one server_rec.  The first ap_context_t is a general context, and should be used for any data that needs to be long lived.  The second is plog, and is a context 
for log streams, this is reset after each read of the config file.  The final context is ptemp, and is meant to be used for very short lived memory allocation.
\par 
\par }\pard \s15\qc\nowidctlpar\adjustright {Data Type Changes
\par }\pard \s15\fi720\nowidctlpar\adjustright {
\par There are other changes for module authors.  These are primarily namespace pr
otection changes.  A number of the data types were re-named when they were moved into the Apache Portable Run-Time(APR).  The list is not long, but not all of the changes have been documented yet.  A quick list of the most obvious changes follows.
\par 
\par table   -->    ap_table_t
\par pool    -->    ap_context_t   (This was done for multiple reasons, which can be \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab reviewed by learning about APR.)
\par array_header   -->    ap_array_header_t
\par table_entry     -->     ap_table_entry
\par 
\par These are the most obvious and the
 most-used data types that have been renamed; however, if when compiling your module you find other standard Apache types that are not available, you should surround the original name with ap_ and _t.  This change was made because these types have been mo
ved out of Apache where only Apache modules could use them, and they have been moved into APR, where non-Apache programs have access to them, and they must be namespace protected.
\par 
\par }\pard \s15\qc\nowidctlpar\adjustright {Thread Safeness Issues
\par 
\par }\pard \s15\fi720\nowidctlpar\adjustright {There is another type of change that module authors 
need to be aware of.  Apache 1.3 on Unix was a process based server, which meant that the modules were protected from data corruption between requests by the nature of processes.  Apache 2.0 has multiple options for running.  It is possible for Apache 2.0
 
on Unix to run in a threaded mode if the operating system supports it.  Because module authors do not know which mode Apache will be run in, all modules should be written to be thread safe.  A  complete discussion of how to write thread safe programs goes
 beyond the scope of this presentation, but it will cover some of the basics.
\par In general, global data in a threaded program should be avoided. When is this not true?  If the global data is initialized in the post-config step, and then never modified again, 
then your module is still safe for Apache 2.0.  Why is this?  The post-config step is run in the parent process, when the program is still running in single threaded mode.  Why is global data bad?  Global variables are shared by all of the threads in a pr
ocess, and they can interfere with each other.
\par Do not assume that the same thread will handle the request all the way through its processing.  People are currently working on making Apache use Asynchronous I/O.  When this work is completed, it is likely tha
t one thread will start processing a request and different threads will run each of the hooks as required.
\par If your module is not thread safe, there are two ways to handle it.  The best way to deal with a non-thread safe module is to correct the problem and
 make the module thread-safe.  This is not always easy to do, so there is of course another alternative.  The module writer can lock a function specific mutex variable everytime a hook is entered and unlock it when the hook is done executing.  There are s
o
me disadvantages to this approach.  The first is speed, because each hook can only have one thread in it at each time, many threads will be kept blocking waiting for the mutex to become available.  This will have a serious effect on your performance, and 
s
hould be taken into account when writing the modules, the amount of code between the locks should be as small as possible.  The other disadvantage to this method is that if the function ever exits without unlocking the mutex variable, any other threads wi
t
hin that process will be unable to continue processing requests which require that module.  However, we realize that even with these disadvantages, some modules will have no choice but to use the locking mechanism.  To this end, we have provided locking m
echanisms in APR.  The use of these API's is beyond the scope of this presentation, but it is well documented in APR.
\par }\pard \s15\qc\fi720\nowidctlpar\adjustright {
\par }\pard \s15\qc\nowidctlpar\adjustright {APR Functions
\par }\pard \s15\qc\fi720\nowidctlpar\adjustright {
\par }\pard \s15\fi720\nowidctlpar\adjustright {The final modification involves APR and how tightly it is integrated into Apache 2.0.  In Apache 1.3, a module could take o
ver the socket or file and write directly to that primitive.  This is no longer the case with Apache 2.0.  Because a good portion of the platform dependant code was moved into APR, modules are required to use the writing primitives which Apache provides. 
 In turn, these primitives will call APR routines to make the correct system call on each platform.
\par 
\par }\pard \s15\qc\nowidctlpar\adjustright {Dig into a module
\par 
\par }\pard \s15\fi735\nowidctlpar\adjustright {The easiest way to figure out how to port modules is to actually try to port one.  Apache 2.0 modules do not differ that much from Apache
 1.3 modules, but the differences are important.  The biggest difference is that the modules must be thread safe.  It is strongly suggested that when porting modules you also port all of the system calls to APR.  This will increase your modules' performan
c
e on all platforms and more tightly integrate your code with Apache.  Using APR will also allow your module to work on most if not all Apache 2.0 supported platforms.  All of the changes to 2.0 modules were made for a reason, either to allow us to modify 
the base without affecting the modules, or to make the modules more powerful.  Please explore these changes and make more suggestions for ways that we can improve the capabilities of Apache modules.
\par }}